Git

Git is a distributed version control system created by Linus Torvalds in 2005. It tracks changes to files over time, allowing developers to review history, experiment on branches, and collaborate without a central server.

Repositories and Commits

A Git repository is a directory whose history Git records. A commit is an immutable snapshot of the project at a point in time, identified by a cryptographic hash. Each commit points to its parent, forming a directed acyclic graph of history.

The Three Areas

Git organizes work into three conceptual areas:

- The working tree, where files are edited

- The staging area (or index), where changes are prepared for a commit

- The repository, where committed snapshots live

The command git add moves changes into the staging area, and git commit records them.

Branching

Branches in Git are lightweight pointers to commits. Creating a branch is nearly instantaneous, which encourages small, focused lines of development. Merging combines histories; when the same lines have changed in both branches, a merge conflict must be resolved manually.

Working with Remotes

A remote is another copy of the repository, often hosted on a service such as GitHub. Commands like git push, git pull, and git fetch synchronize work between local and remote repositories.

